    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "An orally administered OCF3-containing compound crossed the BBB with high brain exposure in rats, providing direct evidence that trifluoromethoxy-substituted aromatics can achieve meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "A fused heterocycle with a methoxybenzamide moiety exhibited a moderate brain uptake index of 59% in rats, indicating reasonable first-pass brain delivery despite structural complexity.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "A more polar methoxyphenyl compound with a primary amide and succinimide showed practically no brain influx in rats, but this contradictory evidence is less transferable because the query lacks HBDs and has lower TPSA.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Pass: the query is a neutral, low-TPSA, no-HBD isatin with favorable passive diffusion properties, and the supportive in vivo brain-penetration evidence from OCF3- and methoxyphenyl-containing analogues outweighs the less transferable poor-penetration data from the more polar QCA scaffold.",
